@@ -69,6 +69,25 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+Use :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` to pretty print a string containing JSON. The output is the JSON re-serialized over multiple lines with syntax highlighting (set ``highlight=False`` to disable it), and the indentation defaults to 4 spaces (use the ``indent`` parameter to change it)::
+
+    console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same renderable is available as :class:`~rich.json.JSON`, so you can print it or log it directly with :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` or :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log`. The renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing crops lines wider than the console to its width. To keep long lines whole, enable soft wrapping, for example ``console.print(JSON(json_text), soft_wrap=True)``.
+
+There is also a package level function, which prints via the global console::
+
+    from rich import print_json
+
+    print_json(json_text)
+
+From the command line, the ``rich.json`` module pretty prints a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
